CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) as a surface interaction model. An incoming light ray strikes a green surface at a point, where the surface normal n points upward and two ray vectors p and q define the local surface coordinate frame. Multiple outgoing arrows emanating in a hemisphere of directions represent the scattered radiance, and the governing equation shows that the reflected intensity depends on the incident radiance, surface orientation, wavelength, and surface material properties. The spread of outgoing rays captures the fact that real surfaces scatter light across many directions rather than reflecting it specularly into a single one. This figure motivates the BRDF as the general mathematical model that describes how a surface converts incoming irradiance into outgoing radiance across all viewing angles.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Large flat green parallelogram representing the surface patch (drawn with a thick black outline, filled bright green)
  - Surface hit point at the center of the patch, marked by a small black ellipse (foreshortened circle) lying in the plane
  - Black surface-normal arrow n rising vertically from the hit point
  - Small black right-angle (perpendicularity) marker between the normal and the surface plane, just right of the hit point
  - Black tangent vector arrow p lying in the surface plane, pointing up-left on screen (toward the incoming ray's azimuth)
  - Black tangent vector arrow q lying in the surface plane, pointing up-right on screen, roughly 90° from p about the normal
  - Thick incoming light ray ℓ_in arriving from the upper-left, drawn as a tapering bar with a yellow→orange→red gradient along its length, terminating at the hit point
  - Short red arrowhead at the end of the incoming ray, touching the surface point
  - Hemisphere of outgoing green arrows radiating from the hit point in many directions above the plane, with arrow lengths proportional to reflected radiance
  - One prominent dark-green outgoing arrow (the longest, near the mirror direction) labelled ℓ_out
  - A lighter, semi-transparent green arrow just left of the main outgoing arrow (a nearby sample direction in the lobe)
  - Label ℓ_in(λ) in italic serif near the top-left, alongside the incoming ray
  - Label ℓ_out = F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q) in italic serif at the upper-right, at the tip of the main outgoing arrow
  - Bold labels n, p, q next to their respective arrows

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World units: surface plane is a square 8 × 8 patch in the XZ plane (y = 0) centered on the origin; the hit point is exactly at the origin. The patch is filled bright green #66EE00 with a 4–5 px black (#000000) outline, and in the default axonometric pose it reads as a wide parallelogram (long axis roughly screen-horizontal, receding edge sloping up-right) occupying the lower 2/3 of the frame. Hit point marked by a black ellipse outline of radius 0.25 lying flat in the plane. Normal n: black arrow from origin along +Y, length 1.3, shaft radius 0.045, cone head 0.28 long; label 'n' in bold just above-left of its head. Right-angle marker: small black L, 0.28 units, in the plane containing +Y and +X, placed just to the +X side of the origin. Tangent p: black arrow in the plane, length 1.1, pointing toward azimuth 135° (screen up-left), label 'p' outside its head. Tangent q: black arrow in the plane, length 1.1, pointing toward azimuth 45° (screen up-right), i.e. 90° from p about the normal; label 'q' outside its head. Incoming ray ℓ_in: a thick tapered bar (radius ~0.16 at the far end narrowing to ~0.05) running from world (-3.2, 4.4, 1.6) down to the origin — i.e. arriving from the upper-left at about 50–55° above the surface, on the p side — coloured with a linear gradient yellow #FFE000 at the far end → orange #FF8C00 mid → red #FF2200 at the surface, capped by a red arrowhead at the origin; label 'ℓ_in(λ)' floats near the upper-left end of the bar. Outgoing lobe: ~14–18 arrows emanating from the origin, directions sampled roughly uniformly over the upper hemisphere (including several low, near-grazing arrows that skim just above the plane in the foreground, as in the source). Arrow length L(ω_o) = 0.55 + 2.2 · f_r(ω_i, ω_o) · cos θ_i, normalized so the longest arrow is 2.6 units; f_r = k_d/π + k_s (cos α)^s with α the angle between ω_o and the mirror reflection r = 2(n·ω_i)n − ω_i, and s = 2/roughness² − 2 (roughness slider), k_d = roughness, k_s = 1 − roughness. Lobe arrows are medium green #46C020 with ~0.7 opacity; the single longest arrow (nearest the mirror direction) is opaque dark green #1A7A1A with a larger head, and one neighbouring arrow is drawn light translucent green #7ADB6B as in the source. Label 'ℓ_out = F(ℓ_in, n, λ, p, q)' in italic serif sits at the tip of the dark-green arrow, upper-right of the frame, and tracks that tip as the incoming ray is dragged. All text black.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
