    }
  }
]

AXIS ANGLES: null — null means the source is a flat 2D figure; pass axisScreenAngles: null to setCameraView for a front-on view.

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Use an orthographic camera in a standard isometric-style pose to reproduce the parallel-edged parallelogram of the source; orbit rotates azimuth freely and clamps elevation to 5°–80° so the plane never flips. Draw the surface as a THREE.Mesh plane with a separate thick black LineSegments/Line2 border (linewidth via Line2/LineMaterial so it survives on all platforms). Build each arrow as a reusable group (CylinderGeometry shaft + ConeGeometry head) and update only scale/quaternion per frame — pool ~20 arrow groups and recompute lengths in a single pass each frame for real-time lobe updates. The incoming ray bar uses a vertex-coloured tapered cylinder (or a CylinderGeometry with radiusTop > radiusBottom and per-vertex colours yellow→orange→red). Labels are CSS2DRenderer/HTML overlays anchored to world points so the serif math typography matches the source and stays screen-facing; the ℓ_out label re-anchors to the current dominant arrow tip each frame. Dragging the incoming ray: raycast the pointer onto a virtual unit hemisphere centred at the origin, clamp the elevation to ≥ 8° above the plane, and drive both the ray bar and the lobe from the resulting ω_i. Render the translucent lobe arrows with depthWrite:false and sort back-to-front to avoid transparency artifacts. Add a faint hemispherical wireframe (very low opacity) only while dragging, to give the drag a visible domain.

